1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 Nf6 { Black develops and attacks e4, planning to give the f4 pawn back later if needed. }
4. e5 { Chases the knight away from f6 before it takes e4, and gains space. }
4... Nh5 ( 4... Qe7 5. Qe2 { Offers a trade that Black cannot afford: the queen on e7 blocks the bishop and the knight has to move again. } 5... Nd5 6. c4 { Kicks the knight again and wins time. The extra tempi are worth more than the pawn. } 6... Nb6 7. d4 )
5. Be2 { Prepares to castle and lines up on the knight on h5, which has few safe squares. }
5... d6
6. d4 { Takes the centre and supports the pawn on e5, so Black's knight on h5 has to find a way back into play. }
6... g5 { Black tries to keep the f4 pawn, but it creates holes on the kingside. }
7. Nc3 { Develops and prepares Nd2 or Qd3 to pile up on the pawns. Do not rush to win the pawn back: the knight on h5 is stranded. }
7... h6 8. Qd3 { Develops the queen and eyes the f-file, ready for long castling. Plan: castle long, then use the stranded knight on h5 and the open f-file. If Black leaves the book, win f4 back only when it is safe. } 8... Bg7 *
